Model a deployment pipeline.

A PipelineStage represents one step (e.g. "build", "test", "deploy"):

Has a name and a function/callable that runs the actual work — assume the callable returns True on success, False on failure (don't worry about what the work actually does — a stage might just be given a lambda for testing).
run() executes that callable and returns whether it succeeded.

A Pipeline runs a sequence of stages:

add_stage(stage) — adds a stage to the sequence.
run() — executes stages in order. If a stage fails, the pipeline must stop immediately — later stages should not run.
After running, a read-only status — "success" if all stages ran and passed, "failed" if it stopped early, "not run" if run() was never called.
last_failed_stage — the name of the stage that caused a failure, or None if none failed (or it hasn't run yet).
Keep a record of which stages actually executed (for a report).

A pipeline with zero stages should be considered a valid success (nothing to fail).